  { n:1, summary:"During the days when the judges ruled, a famine drives a family from Bethlehem-judah to sojourn in Moab: Elimelech, his wife Naomi, and their two sons Mahlon and Chilion. Elimelech dies, the sons marry Moabite women — Orpah and Ruth — and after about ten years both sons die as well. Hearing that the LORD has visited His people in giving them bread, Naomi sets out for Judah with her daughters in law. She urges both to return to their mothers' houses, since she is too old to bear more sons; Orpah eventually goes back, but Ruth cleaves to her: whither thou goest, I will go; thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God. The two arrive in Bethlehem at the beginning of barley harvest, and Naomi tells the astonished city to call her not Naomi but Mara, for I went out full, and the LORD hath brought me home again empty.",
    context:"In the days when the judges ruled, between Bethlehem-judah and the country of Moab; the book does not name its author, though tradition has often linked it to Samuel.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ruth 1:16", text:"And Ruth said, Entreat me not to leave thee, or to return from following after thee: for whither thou goest, I will go; and where thou lodgest, I will lodge: thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God:" },
      { ref:"Ruth 1:17", text:"Where thou diest, will I die, and there will I be buried: the LORD do so to me, and more also, if ought but death part thee and me." },
      { ref:"Ruth 1:20", text:"And she said unto them, Call me not Naomi, call me Mara: for the Almighty hath dealt very bitterly with me." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Loyal love commits for the long road, even when following God means leaving everything familiar.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why did Elimelech's family leave Bethlehem for Moab?", choices:["A famine in the land","An invading army","A family quarrel","To buy land"], a:0, why:"There was a famine in the land, so the man of Bethlehem-judah went to sojourn in the country of Moab with his wife and two sons." },
      { q:"What did Ruth give up, according to her own words, in returning with Naomi?", choices:["Only her inheritance in Moab","Her own people and gods — thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God","Her claim to Elimelech's field","Her right to remarry in Moab"], a:1, why:"Ruth said, whither thou goest, I will go; thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God — a whole-life commitment to Naomi's land and Lord." },
      { q:"What does Naomi's renaming herself Mara reveal about how she read her losses?", choices:["She blamed the people of Bethlehem","She planned to return to Moab","She felt the Almighty had dealt very bitterly with her, going out full and coming home empty","She believed her sons would rise again"], a:2, why:"Call me not Naomi, call me Mara: for the Almighty hath dealt very bitterly with me... I went out full, and the LORD hath brought me home again empty." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"Naomi has a kinsman of Elimelech's family, a mighty man of wealth named Boaz. Ruth volunteers to glean ears of corn in any field where she may find grace, and her hap is to light on part of the field belonging to Boaz. He greets his reapers with a blessing, notices the stranger, and learns she is the Moabitess who came back with Naomi. Boaz tells her to stay by his maidens, has charged his young men not to touch her, and invites her to drink from the vessels his servants have drawn. Bowing, Ruth marvels that he takes knowledge of a stranger; Boaz answers that her loyalty to Naomi has been fully reported, and prays a full reward from the LORD God of Israel, under whose wings she is come to trust. At mealtime he feeds her, orders handfuls of purpose dropped for her, and she beats out about an ephah of barley for Naomi — who learns the gleaner's benefactor is one of their next kinsmen.",
    context:"During the barley harvest in Bethlehem, soon after Naomi and Ruth returned from Moab with nothing.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ruth 2:12", text:"The LORD recompense thy work, and a full reward be given thee of the LORD God of Israel, under whose wings thou art come to trust." },
      { ref:"Ruth 2:16", text:"And let fall also some of the handfuls of purpose for her, and leave them, that she may glean them, and rebuke her not." },
      { ref:"Ruth 2:20", text:"And Naomi said unto her daughter in law, Blessed be he of the LORD, who hath not left off his kindness to the living and to the dead." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Kindness given in hard seasons is often God's quiet provision; He rewards those who take shelter in Him.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"About how much barley did Ruth beat out after her first day of gleaning?", choices:["A handful","A sheaf","A seah","About an ephah"], a:3, why:"She gleaned in the field until even, and beat out that she had gleaned: and it was about an ephah of barley." },
      { q:"What reason did Boaz give for taking special notice of Ruth?", choices:["It had been fully showed him all she had done for Naomi since her husband died","Naomi had sent him a letter","The reapers asked him to hire her","She paid him for the privilege"], a:0, why:"Boaz answered, It hath fully been shewed me, all that thou hast done unto thy mother in law since the death of thine husband." },
      { q:"What picture does Boaz use to describe Ruth's relationship with the LORD God of Israel?", choices:["A vine planted by rivers of water","Coming to trust under the LORD's wings","A lamp lit in darkness","A stone laid on a foundation"], a:1, why:"Boaz blessed her: The LORD recompense thy work, and a full reward be given thee of the LORD God of Israel, under whose wings thou art come to trust." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"Naomi now seeks rest for Ruth. Knowing Boaz is winnowing barley at his threshing floor that night, she sends Ruth, washed and anointed, to lie quietly at his feet after he has eaten — a humble appeal for him to act as her near kinsman. At midnight he startles awake; Ruth asks him to spread his skirt over his handmaid, for thou art a near kinsman. Boaz blesses her for showing more kindness at the latter end than at the beginning, not following young men whether poor or rich; all the city knows she is a virtuous woman. He promises to do all she requires, though a kinsman nearer than himself stands first. If that man declines, Boaz vows to do the kinsman's part as the LORD liveth. Before dawn he sends her home with six measures of barley — Go not empty unto thy mother in law — and Naomi counsels her to sit still, for the man will not rest until the matter is finished that day.",
    context:"During the barley harvest in Bethlehem, on a night of winnowing at the threshing floor, as Naomi arranged for Ruth's future.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ruth 3:9", text:"And he said, Who art thou? And she answered, I am Ruth thine handmaid: spread therefore thy skirt over thine handmaid; for thou art a near kinsman." },
      { ref:"Ruth 3:11", text:"And now, my daughter, fear not; I will do to thee all that thou requirest: for all the city of my people doth know that thou art a virtuous woman." },
      { ref:"Ruth 3:18", text:"Then said she, Sit still, my daughter, until thou know how the matter will fall: for the man will not be in rest, until he have finished the thing this day." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Virtue and patience are noticed; God can be trusted to settle the matters we commit to Him.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What did Boaz measure out for Ruth before she returned to the city?", choices:["Twelve measures of wheat","Two ephahs of corn","Six measures of barley","A bag of silver"], a:2, why:"He measured six measures of barley, and laid it on her: for he said, Go not empty unto thy mother in law." },
      { q:"What complication did Boaz name when he promised to act for Ruth?", choices:["He was not actually related to Elimelech","The elders would not approve the marriage","The harvest had not yet ended","There was a kinsman nearer than he, who had the first right to act"], a:3, why:"Boaz said, it is true that I am thy near kinsman: howbeit there is a kinsman nearer than I." },
      { q:"What does Boaz's praise of Ruth — all the city doth know that thou art a virtuous woman — teach about reputation?", choices:["Consistent goodness becomes known in a community and speaks for itself","Only the wealthy can be virtuous","Reputation matters more than character","Virtue should be hidden from others"], a:0, why:"Boaz told Ruth not to fear, because all the city of his people knew she was a virtuous woman — her conduct had earned trust." }
    ] },
  { n:4, summary:"Boaz takes his case to the gate of Bethlehem. He gathers ten elders just as the nearer kinsman passes by, and presents the matter: Naomi is selling the parcel of land that belonged to Elimelech, and the kinsman has the first right to redeem it. The man agrees — until Boaz adds that buying the field includes marrying Ruth the Moabitess, the wife of the dead, to raise up the name of the dead upon his inheritance. Lest he mar his own inheritance, the kinsman yields his right, confirmed by the custom of drawing off a shoe. Before the elders and all the people, Boaz declares he has bought all that was Elimelech's, Chilion's, and Mahlon's, and has purchased Ruth to be his wife. The witnesses bless the couple as like Rachel and Leah, and the LORD grants conception: Ruth bears a son. The women tell Naomi he is a restorer of her life, and the neighbors name the child Obed — the father of Jesse, the father of David.",
    context:"At the gate of Bethlehem, where legal matters were decided before the elders of the city, shortly after the threshing-floor night.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ruth 4:10", text:"Moreover Ruth the Moabitess, the wife of Mahlon, have I purchased to be my wife, to raise up the name of the dead upon his inheritance, that the name of the dead be not cut off from among his brethren, and from the gate of his place: ye are witnesses this day." },
      { ref:"Ruth 4:14", text:"And the women said unto Naomi, Blessed be the LORD, which hath not left thee this day without a kinsman, that his name may be famous in Israel." },
      { ref:"Ruth 4:17", text:"And the women her neighbours gave it a name, saying, There is a son born to Naomi; and they called his name Obed: he is the father of Jesse, the father of David." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The Lord redeems what loss has emptied, turning bitter years into a lineage of blessing.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many elders did Boaz take as witnesses at the gate?", choices:["Three","Ten","Seven","Twelve"], a:1, why:"Boaz took ten men of the elders of the city and said, Sit ye down here." },
      { q:"Why did the nearer kinsman change his mind about redeeming Elimelech's land?", choices:["Naomi raised the price","The elders advised against it","Redeeming included marrying Ruth to raise up the name of the dead, marring his own inheritance","He discovered the field was barren"], a:2, why:"Boaz explained the purchase included Ruth the Moabitess, to raise up the name of the dead; the kinsman said, I cannot redeem it for myself, lest I mar mine own inheritance." },
      { q:"What does the book's closing genealogy — Obed, Jesse, David — reveal about the meaning of Ruth's story?", choices:["It shows Moabite law was superior to Israelite law","It proves Naomi returned to wealth","It records why Ruth left Bethlehem","The Lord wove a faithful outsider's loyalty into the family line of Israel's greatest king"], a:3, why:"The women named the child Obed, the father of Jesse, the father of David — Ruth's redemption story leads directly to Israel's royal line." }
    ] }
